# PART 0 — Scope, thesis and architecture

## 0.1 The thesis

The starting point is a margin page that values a NEM gas generator as a **flat baseload swap** on a single price path:

$$\Pi_{\text{old}} \approx (F - \mathrm{SRMC}) \times \text{flat volume}$$

This is structurally wrong for a peaker, and the correct frame is:

> The generator **sells $300 cap contracts**, collects the cap **premium** as carry, and **turns on to defend the caps** when there is risk of spot exceeding $300. Its margin is the cap premium plus the energy spread it captures, less the cost of generation and the cost of being available.

The reason is structural, not stylistic:

- A baseload swap is a **linear** payoff in spot. A flat block is hedged by a flat block.
- A gas peaker's physical output is a **convex, option-like** payoff: it generates — and earns — only in intervals where $S > \mathrm{SRMC}$. In payoff terms it is a *strip of spark-spread options*, which in the NEM is economically a *strip of ~$300 caps*.
- You hedge an instrument with another instrument of the **same payoff shape**. The cap matches the asset; the swap does not.

A swap hedge on a peaker leaves you short a linear instrument while long a convex asset — a shape (gamma) mismatch that systematically misstates margin. The cap collapses that mismatch.

**The one caveat, stated up front.** This framing is most exact for **OCGT peakers** — and any unit whose SRMC sits high on the price-duration curve, so it runs only in the peaks. A **CCGT running mid-merit or baseload** legitimately carries a swap on its baseload block *plus* caps on its peaking tail. The general principle: **the hedge instrument must match the physical run profile**, and the run profile is determined by where SRMC sits relative to the price-duration curve. The cap-based method is the peaker limit; the mixed case is a linear combination of a swap leg and a cap leg. Both legs are carried explicitly throughout this document.

## 0.2 The five modules and what each one does

**Module A — Cap-based margin valuation (Part I).** Simulates 5-minute joint power–gas paths, dispatches each unit under a non-anticipative commitment policy, and evaluates the master margin equation per path. Outputs a *distribution* of quarterly margin per unit, decomposed into energy spread, premium carry, cap payouts and commitment drag.

**Module B — Valuation & trading pack (Part II).** The presentation and decision layer: seven tiers of analytics that turn the distribution into a trade ticket a desk will act on, plus the credibility charts that make a sophisticated desk believe it.

**Module C — Hedging supply curve (Part III).** Takes each *owner's* netted margin distribution and extracts the **reservation price** of each hedge instrument — the level at which the owner is just willing to sell the marginal MW. Sweeping price traces a supply curve; summing horizontally across owners and adding the retailer demand side predicts the clearing premium, which is then read against the ASX screen.

**Module D — Position inference & calibration (Part IV).** Recovers, from public data, the positions generators actually hold — three estimators of increasing rigour, reconciled against open interest — and then tunes the Module C risk parameters so predicted supply matches inferred actuals. This is the empirical discipline that gives the supply curve standing to predict.

**Module E — Reference implementation (Part V).** A runnable Python package implementing Modules C and D against a defined engine seam, with a synthetic stub engine so the pipeline runs end-to-end before the production engine is wired in.

