# PART I — MODULE A: Cap-based margin valuation

Work at native dispatch resolution — 5-minute, post-5MS.

## I.1 The master per-interval equation

The generator's financial position in interval $i$ is **physical asset + short cap + premium carry**. As a single expression:

```
π_i = Δt · [  G_i·(L·S_i − SRMC_i)      ← physical spark spread on dispatched MWh
            − N·max(S_i − K, 0)         ← short-cap payout (financial, paid regardless of G)
            + N·p                       ← cap premium accrual
           ]
```

$$\boxed{\;\pi_i = \Delta t\Big[\,G_i\,(L\,S_i - \mathrm{SRMC}_i)\;-\;N\,\max(S_i-K,0)\;+\;N\,p\,\Big]\;}$$

Over the quarter, with the commitment costs the per-interval term does not capture:

$$\Pi \;=\; \sum_i \pi_i \;-\; \sum_{\text{starts}} \mathrm{SC} \;-\; \mathrm{FOM}$$

Minimum-load running is handled by setting $G_i = P_{\min}$ in "ready-but-idle" intervals — the unit is synchronised, burning fuel, waiting to defend. $\mathrm{SC}$ is charged once per off→on transition. This is the unit-commitment form of commitment drag (min-load, start, no-load, FOM).

**Three properties of this equation, each of which resolves a common modelling error:**

**(1) The cap term does not depend on $G_i$.** $\max(S_i - K, 0)$ is owed whether or not you generate. Generating does not *earn* the cap; it *physically offsets* the liability. This is the precise meaning of "defending the cap."

**(2) Optimal dispatch is $L\,S_i > \mathrm{SRMC}_i$, full stop.** Differentiate $\pi_i$ with respect to $G_i$: the marginal MWh is worth $L S_i - \mathrm{SRMC}_i$, and the cap term is constant in $G_i$. So you run flat out when $L S_i > \mathrm{SRMC}_i$ and stay at zero otherwise — *independent of the cap*. **The cap changes the commitment decision (whether to be online and ready), not the marginal dispatch decision.** Models that let the cap alter the dispatch rule are wrong.

**(3) The premium is collected in every interval.** $+N p \Delta t$ appears in all branches. The intuitive distinction — premium is "clean" when $S < 300$, "consumed" when $S > 300$ — is an *interpretation* of the same cash flow, not a different cash flow. Keep $+Np$ everywhere or the margin is biased.

## I.2 Case recovery — every dispatch case falls out of the one equation

Set $L = 1$ for clarity (MLF restored in §I.5). Each hand-written case is a special evaluation of $\pi_i$:

**Case A — $S < 300$, unit off ($G = 0$).**
$$\pi = \Delta t\,[\,0 - 0 + Np\,] = \Delta t\,N p$$
Pure premium accrual.

**Case B — $S < 300$, unit on ($G > 0$, because $S > \mathrm{SRMC}$) — "under-cap, optimal."**
$$\pi = \Delta t\,[\,G(S - \mathrm{SRMC}) + Np\,]$$
Spark spread **plus** premium. Note SRMC is per *dispatched* MWh, i.e. multiplied by $G$.

**Case C — $S > 300$, defended ($G = N$) — "over-cap, generated."**
$$\pi = \Delta t\,[\,N(S - \mathrm{SRMC}) - N(S - 300) + Np\,] = \Delta t\,[\,N(300 - \mathrm{SRMC}) + Np\,]$$
The effective realised energy price is **capped at \$300**, less SRMC, plus premium.

**Case D — $S > 300$, *not* defended ($G = 0$) — the disaster case.**
$$\pi = \Delta t\,[\,0 - N(S-300) + Np\,] = \Delta t\,[\,Np - N(S-300)\,]$$
You owe the full cap payout with no physical offset. With $S$ able to reach the MPC, this is the dominant risk in the entire problem.

**Case E — partial defence ($0 < G < N$)** is the case rarely written down and where real units actually live. It is not a separate formula; it is the general equation, and §I.3 decomposes it.

## I.3 The covered / uncovered decomposition — and why $N$ vs capacity is the real risk lever

When $S > K$ and the unit generates $G$ (not necessarily equal to $N$), rearrange the spike-interval payoff:

$$\boxed{\;G\,S - N(S-K) \;=\; \underbrace{K\,G}_{\text{covered MW earn }\$300} \;-\; \underbrace{(N-G)\,(S-K)}_{\text{uncovered MW lose }(S-K)}\;}$$

This is the clean statement of cap defence:

- **Covered megawatts** — the $G$ you actually get online — each earn a net **\$300**, regardless of how high $S$ goes. The upside above \$300 was sold for the premium.
- **Uncovered megawatts** — $(N-G)$, the caps sold but not backed by generation — each **lose $(S-K)$**. At the MPC this is catastrophic.
- If $G > N$ (capacity beyond the caps sold), the term flips sign to $+(G-N)(S-K)$: the **merchant tail** — open capacity capturing the full spike. This is the upside retained by *not* selling caps on 100% of capacity.

**Implication for sizing $N$.** $N$ is a risk-management choice, not a given. Selling caps on more MW than you can *reliably* generate during a spike manufactures the uncovered tail. The trade-off is premium income (rises with $N$) against tail risk (rises with $N -$ reliable capacity). The Eydeland–Wolyniec load-cap/insurance result applies directly: if the dominant concern is rare large losses, the optimal short-cap volume is bounded by the volume you can physically defend with high probability — typically a **haircut to nameplate** reflecting forced-outage rate and start reliability, **not** nameplate itself. That haircut is quantified by the spike capture rate (§II Tier 3.2) and becomes the hard constraint $\bar q_{cap}$ in the supply-curve program (§III.5).

## I.4 The MLF basis on the hedge

Restore $L$. In a fully defended spike ($G = N$):

$$L\,N\,S - N(S-K) \;=\; N\,\big(K - (1-L)\,S\big)$$

A "covered" position is therefore **not** a perfect \$300 lock. With $L < 1$ you leak $(1-L)\,S$ per MW *even when fully defended* — at high $S$ this is material. The cap is a regional-reference-node instrument; your energy is paid at the loss-adjusted local node. The residual $(1-L)S$ is genuine basis risk that a flat-swap model never surfaces, because it never modelled the cap leg separately.

Numerically: at $L = 0.95$ and $S = \$10{,}000$, the leak is $\$500$/MW/h — larger than the entire defended margin $(300 - \mathrm{SRMC})$. **Refresh MLFs whenever AEMO republishes them**; the spike-interval leverage is why this matters more than the annual 1–2% MLF drift suggests.

## I.5 Why cap, not swap — the comparison done properly

Two distinct failures of the swap frame.

### I.5.1 Shape mismatch (the structural failure)

The swap-hedged P&L is:

$$\Pi_{\text{swap}} = \sum_i G_i\,\Delta t\,(L S_i - \mathrm{SRMC}) \;-\; \sum_i V\,(S_i - F)\,\Delta t \;-\;(\text{commitment, FOM})$$

with swap volume $V$ flat across **all** intervals. The asset generates $G_i$ concentrated in high-$S$ intervals; the swap subtracts $V(S_i - F)$ in **every** interval. In a spike the swap loses $\approx V S_i$ while the asset earns only $G_i \le C$ worth of spread. If $V > C$ you are **net short the spike** — over-hedged in exactly the tail the asset was built to capture.

The cap, by contrast: pays only in the spike (matching when the asset is exposed), is sized at $N \le C$ (matching deliverable capacity), and is struck at the tail. Same shape as the asset.

### I.5.2 Wrong reference price (the measurement failure)

Define the **capture price** (generation-weighted):

$$P_{\text{cap}} = \frac{\sum_i S_i\,G_i}{\sum_i G_i}, \qquad E = \sum_i G_i\,\Delta t \ \ \text{(generated MWh)}$$

The merchant energy margin is then, for constant SRMC:

$$\sum_i G_i\,\Delta t\,(L S_i - \mathrm{SRMC}) \;=\; E\,\big(L\,P_{\text{cap}} - \mathrm{SRMC}\big)$$

**Headline margin = capture − SRMC on generation-weighted energy, not time-weighted flat energy.** A peaker's capture price sits far above the baseload time-weighted average $\bar S$, because it generates only when prices are high. A flat swap locks margin against $\bar S$ — the *wrong* price. The swap model hedges the wrong shape **and** measures against the wrong average. The cap-based method values the asset at its true capture price and prices the tail at the traded cap premium.

### I.5.3 The economics of selling caps (why it is a business, not just a hedge)

Total premium collected per quarter: $N p H$. Expected payout under the physical measure: $N H\,\mathbb{E}_P[\max(S-K,0)]$. Expected profit on the cap leg:

$$N\,H\,\big(p - \mathbb{E}_P[\max(S-K,0)]\big) \;=\; N\,H \times (\text{risk premium})$$

Cap **buyers** — retailers hedging their own short-tail exposure — pay up for insurance, so typically $p > \mathbb{E}_P[\max(S-K,0)]$: the seller earns a **positive risk premium** *and* reduces earnings variance. The cap-based margin therefore has two sources the swap margin cannot represent: the carry/risk-premium on the short cap, and the variance reduction from collaring the tail. The **observable ASX cap forward $p$ is the market's price of that tail**, and it is the calibration anchor for the whole tail of the price simulator (§I.8).

## I.6 NEM market mechanics that bind the model

Hard constraints the price simulator and the margin accounting must respect. Current values as at mid-2026; refresh annually.

| Mechanic | Value / behaviour | Modelling consequence |
|---|---|---|
| **5-minute settlement (5MS)** | energy settles per 5-min dispatch interval | simulate at 5-min; spikes are often *single-interval*, which is why availability and ramp dominate |
| **Market Price Cap (MPC)** | **\$20,300/MWh** FY2025-26 → **\$23,200/MWh** from 1 July 2026 (further step FY2027-28) | truncate the simulated distribution at the MPC for the relevant period; this sets the magnitude of the uncovered-tail loss |
| **Market Floor** | −\$1,000/MWh | floor the distribution |
| **Cumulative Price Threshold → APC** | rolling 7-day price sum breaching CPT (≈\$2.23M) triggers an Administered Price Period capping prices at the **APC of \$600/MWh** until cleared | a *state-dependent ceiling on the cumulative window*; truncates sustained scarcity. Without it, a spike-only model overstates prolonged-scarcity value |
| **\$300 cap strike ≠ APC** | ASX cap strike is \$300 by product convention; the APC used to be \$300 but is now \$600 | do not conflate them — a common and expensive error |
| **Regional reference price** | caps and swaps settle on the region's RRP; energy paid at RRP × MLF at the connection point | cap products are **quarterly by region**; the forward cap curve is quarter × region |

## I.7 The dispatch & commitment policy — where overvaluation hides

This is the crux of "as realistic as possible," and the single largest source of inflated valuations.

### I.7.1 The two-layer decision

- **Dispatch (intra-interval):** run iff $L S_i > \mathrm{SRMC}_i$. Trivial *given* you are online.
- **Commitment (anticipatory):** whether to **start** — incurring $\mathrm{SC}$ plus min-load fuel losses — so that you *are* online and able to defend a spike that **has not happened yet**. A decision under uncertainty, made with only the information available at the time (pre-dispatch forecasts, current regime, recent prices) — **not** the realised path.

The commitment layer is a **compound option**: the start cost is the strike on an overlying call written on the strip of spark-spread (cap-defence) payoffs. Shutting down sells that strip and stops the min-load bleed. Commitment drag is the premium paid — in physical fuel and start costs — to hold the defensive option.

### I.7.2 Why perfect-foresight dispatch overvalues

A MILP or DP solved over the **realised** price path has perfect foresight: it starts exactly before each spike, never starts in vain, always synchronises in time. That value is an **upper bound**, not a forecast. Reality breaks it three ways:

1. **Start lead time.** An OCGT needs minutes to ~30 min from cold/warm; a CCGT needs hours. A **single 5-minute** spike can be over before a cold unit synchronises → missed capture, and if short caps, an **uncovered loss**.
2. **Forecast error.** You start on *probability*. Sometimes the spike doesn't materialise → a **false start**: $\mathrm{SC}$ plus min-load losses spent for nothing.
3. **Min up / min down / ramp.** Once on, minimum-up-time forces continued running (more min-load bleed) even as price falls; ramp limits cap how fast $G$ rises within the spike.

The value hierarchy:

```
strip of independent spark-spread options (full optionality, no constraints)   ← upper bound
     ≥  constrained SDP value (start cost, min up/down, ramp)
          ≥  realistic non-anticipative policy value (forecast-driven)         ← model this
```

**The gap between the top and bottom rungs is optionality you do not actually have.** A flat-swap model sits *outside* this chain entirely — it is a mis-specified hedge P&L, not even an upper bound. Target the bottom rung.

### I.7.3 Implementing the policy

**Heuristic commitment rule (good first build).** Over the start-lead-time window $[t, t+\tau_{\text{start}}]$, estimate spike probability $q$ and expected uplift $\Delta$ from the simulated regime/forecast *at time $t$*. Then:

$$\textbf{start if}\quad q\cdot\Delta\cdot(\text{deliverable MW}) \;>\; \mathrm{SC} + \mathbb{E}[\text{min-load loss over }\tau_{\text{start}}]$$

Defend while online; shut down when the forward-looking uplift no longer covers the min-load bleed, respecting min-up-time. Transparent and tunable.

**Stochastic dynamic programming (production-grade).** State $= (\text{on/off},\ \text{time-in-state},\ \text{price regime},\ \text{gas state})$. Solve the value function $V(\text{state}; t)$ backwards; the policy emerges. Critically, **drive the SDP with realistic conditioning information** (pre-dispatch-style forecasts), *not* the realised path — otherwise perfect foresight has been re-introduced through the back door.

A closely related point that matters for the DP formulation: **lumpy costs change the dispatch *pattern*, not just the margin total.** Netting startup and no-load costs against margin *ex post* — while dispatching on SRMC alone — produces systematically wrong run patterns (over-starting and over-stopping), not merely wrong totals. Runtime is an *output* of the optimisation, not an input. Fast-start OCGTs sit close to the myopic SRMC rule; CCGTs and coal diverge substantially.

Either way, the unit is a peaker/cycling asset with a **low at-the-money delta**: far out-of-the-money most of the time, with most of its value coming from the spike distribution — which is precisely what the cap premium prices.

## I.8 The cost stack: SRMC, VOM, FOM, start and no-load

SRMC is the anchor for *both* dispatch and the price at which the generator is indifferent to defending. It is the floor the whole margin sits above.

$$\boxed{\;\mathrm{SRMC}_i = \mathrm{HR}(G_i)\times \big(P^{\text{gas}}_i + T^{\text{gas}}\big) + \mathrm{VOM}\;}$$

- **Heat rate $\mathrm{HR}(G)$** in GJ/MWh sent-out, **load-dependent** (worse at part load). Convert efficiency $\eta$ via $\mathrm{HR} = 3.6/\eta$. OCGT $\eta \approx 33\% \Rightarrow \mathrm{HR} \approx 10.9$ GJ/MWh; CCGT $\eta \approx 50\% \Rightarrow \mathrm{HR} \approx 7.2$ GJ/MWh. Use full-load HR when defending (you run flat out), worse HR for the min-load bleed term. The distinction between **incremental** and **average** heat rate matters: the dispatch decision uses incremental, the fuel-cost total uses average.
- **Delivered gas** $=$ commodity ($P^{\text{gas}}$: Wallumbilla / STTM hub, or contracted GSA) $+$ transport tariff ($T^{\text{gas}}$), in \$/GJ.
- **VOM** in \$/MWh — consumables, water, non-fuel variable maintenance. Scales with output, so it *belongs* inside SRMC.
- **Carbon** where applicable (Safeguard Mechanism liability, ACCU cost) enters as an additional \$/MWh term via the emissions intensity.
- **MLF referencing.** SRMC is a *sent-out* cost; revenue is $L \cdot S$. Compare like with like — either gross the SRMC up by $1/L$ or net the price down by $L$, consistently.
- **Start cost $\mathrm{SC}$ and no-load/min-load fuel are *separate* commitment-drag terms (§I.7), not part of marginal SRMC.**

### The two-threshold framing

There are two distinct decision thresholds and conflating them is a modelling error:

1. **Output-level threshold** — governed by *incremental* SRMC: given the unit is online, produce the marginal MWh iff $L S > \mathrm{SRMC}^{\text{inc}}$.
2. **Start/stop commitment threshold** — governed by the *lumpy* costs: start iff the expected value of being online over the commitment horizon exceeds $\mathrm{SC}$ plus the no-load/min-load burn.

### FOM, and where it belongs

**FOM is fixed operating & maintenance cost** — costs that don't move with generation: staffing, scheduled maintenance contracts, insurance, connection and network charges. Usually quoted in \$/MW/year. It is the counterpart to **VOM**, which *does* scale with output and therefore lives inside SRMC.

The rule: **FOM never touches the marginal dispatch decision** — that remains purely $L S > \mathrm{SRMC}$. It is a sunk period cost that appears only at the bottom of the margin waterfall, determining whether the asset covers its fixed base and earns a positive *net* margin. FOM answers *"is this generator viable,"* not *"should it run this interval."*

### Worked SRMC and the gas–power coupling

OCGT, $\mathrm{HR} = 10.9$, delivered gas \$14/GJ, VOM \$7:

$$\mathrm{SRMC} \approx 10.9 \times 14 + 7 \approx \$160/\text{MWh}$$

At a *tight* gas price of \$20/GJ:

$$\mathrm{SRMC} \approx 10.9 \times 20 + 7 \approx \$225/\text{MWh}$$

Now the defended-spike net is only $300 - 225 = \$75$/MWh. Cap-defence economics **compress as gas rises**, and at roughly **\$27/GJ** SRMC reaches the strike — defence becomes break-even *before* MLF leakage is counted.

This is exactly why **gas and power must be simulated jointly, not independently**: the spike that makes the cap pay is frequently the *same* event that spikes gas, moving SRMC against you precisely when you most need to defend. The consequence propagates all the way through to Module C, where the cap supply curve *stiffens* endogenously in tight-gas regimes (§III.7).

## I.9 Price-path simulation and calibration

Simulate **5-minute, jointly with gas**, then calibrate to the two traded instruments. The key structural insight: the two market anchors pin two *distinct moments* of the distribution.

| Market input | Pins | Model knob |
|---|---|---|
| ASX quarterly **swap** $F_Q$ | the **level** $\mathbb{E}^*[\bar S]$ over the quarter | drift / mean of the price process |
| ASX quarterly **\$300 cap** $p_Q$ | the **tail** $\mathbb{E}^*[\max(S-300,0)]$ | spike intensity & magnitude |

Two equations (level, tail), two knobs (drift, spikes). **The cap premium *is* the calibration target for spike behaviour.** Spike miscalibration is the material risk in this whole model, and the \$300 cap price is the only market-observable anchor for it.

### Process candidates

All valid; choose for tractability versus fidelity.

- **Regime-switching** (normal ~\$50 regime / spike ~\$300+ regime). Natural fit to NEM behaviour and to the cap calibration. Under regime-switching the optimal defence boundary is trivial: defend whenever the price enters the spike regime.
- **Mean-reverting jump-diffusion (MRJD).** Continuous base plus Poisson spikes; calibrate jump intensity and size to the cap.
- **Structural / bid-stack.** Price as residual demand against a supply stack; gas–power correlation emerges *endogenously* through the marginal unit. Heaviest, most internally consistent, and the natural home if you are already modelling the bid stack for the inference layer (§IV.13).
- **Bootstrap / copula resampling** of historical 5-minute blocks. Preserves diurnal and weekly shape and empirical spike clustering; couple power–gas via copula.

### Calibration sequence

1. **Historical 5-minute data** → intra-quarter shape (diurnal, weekly), volatility, mean-reversion speed, spike clustering, and the **power–gas dependence**.
2. **Swap forward** → shift the **level** so the time-weighted simulated mean reproduces $F_Q$.
3. **Cap forward** → tune **spike** parameters so the risk-neutral expected cap payoff reproduces $p_Q$.
4. **Impose NEM rails** → truncate at MPC; apply the CPT→APC administered ceiling on the rolling window; floor at −\$1,000.

Do these **in order**: the level calibration is nearly orthogonal to the tail calibration, but not exactly, so iterate steps 2–3 to convergence.

### Keep the two measures explicit

Calibrate the **tail to $p_Q$ under the pricing measure** for no-arbitrage, but read the **physical-measure** expected payout and the full P&L *distribution* separately. The realised-margin question is a physical-measure question, and **the gap between the two measures is the risk premium being earned**. Conflating them is the single most common way these models lose desk credibility — hence the two-headline-number discipline in Module B.

### An honest caveat on marking illiquid contracts

ASX settlement prices for illiquid back contracts are frequently **marked or derived rather than traded**. This can induce spurious comovement between series. Where you are estimating correlations across the forward curve from settlement data, be aware that standard staleness corrections (Dimson, Scholes–Williams) may be *counterproductive* in this regime — they correct for a lag structure that isn't actually there, and can manufacture the very comovement you were trying to remove.

## I.10 Margin computation and outputs

**Per Monte Carlo path:**

1. Simulate 5-minute $S_i$ and gas → $\mathrm{SRMC}_i$.
2. Run the **non-anticipative commitment + dispatch policy** (§I.7) → $G_i$, starts, min-load intervals.
3. Evaluate $\pi_i$ (master equation) per interval; sum; subtract $\sum \mathrm{SC}$ and FOM → path margin $\Pi$.
4. Record: capture price $P_{\text{cap}}$, generated energy $E$, premium income $N p H$, cap payouts $\sum_i N\max(S_i-300,0)\Delta t$, commitment drag, and the **uncovered-MWh count** (intervals with $S > 300$ and $G < N$).

**Then report across paths — and report the distribution, not just the mean**, because the entire point of the cap frame is the risk profile:

- **Headline margin** per dispatched MWh: $L\,P_{\text{cap}} - \mathrm{SRMC}$, plus the cap-leg contribution $(N p H - \text{cap payouts})/E$.
- **Mean** cap-collared $\Pi$, **and** the **left tail** (5th and 1st percentile, CVaR). The uncovered-spike scenarios are the whole risk story.
- **Decomposition:** energy spread vs premium carry vs cap payouts vs commitment drag — so you can see *where* the margin comes from, and how the swap model misattributed it.

## I.11 Validation and no-arbitrage checks

| Check | Test | Consequence of failure |
|---|---|---|
| **Cap reprice** | simulated cap-payout distribution must reprice the traded $p_Q$ under the pricing measure | tail is mis-calibrated → **no margin number is reliable**; fix before anything else |
| **Swap reprice** | time-weighted simulated mean must reproduce $F_Q$ | level is mis-calibrated |
| **Defence sanity** | in backtest, the policy's $G_i$ during historical spikes should resemble realised plant behaviour (capture rate, start frequency) | if the model "defends" spikes a real OCGT physically couldn't reach, perfect foresight has leaked in |
| **Bound check** | realistic-policy value $\le$ constrained-SDP value $\le$ unconstrained spark-spread-strip value | if the number exceeds the strip bound, there is a bug |
| **MLF / gas refresh** | re-run on current MLFs and gas curves each cycle | stale basis and stale SRMC |

## I.12 Worked example

OCGT. $N = 100$ MW caps sold at $p = \$18$/MWh, $K = 300$, $\mathrm{SRMC} = \$160$/MWh, $L = 1$, one 5-minute interval ($\Delta t = 1/12$ h).

Quarter premium collected: $N p H = 100 \times 18 \times 2184 \approx \$3.93\text{M}$. Per-MW per-interval premium: $18/12 = \$1.50$.

| Scenario | $S$ | $G$ | $\pi_i$ (5-min) | Reading |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **A** Off-peak, unit off | 80 | 0 | $(1/12)(100 \cdot 18) = +\$150$ | pure premium carry |
| **B** Under-cap, dispatched | 250 | 100 | $(1/12)[100(250-160)+1800] = +\$908$ | spark spread **+** premium |
| **C** Spike, **defended** | 5,000 | 100 | $(1/12)[100(300-160)+1800] = +\$1{,}325$ | energy capped at \$300, less SRMC, **+** premium |
| **D** Spike, **undefended** | 5,000 | 0 | $(1/12)[1800 - 100(4700)] = -\$39{,}017$ | full uncovered payout — **one interval** |

Scenario D at the **MPC (\$20,300)**: $\approx (1/12)[1800 - 100(20000)] \approx -\$166{,}500$ for a **single 5-minute interval**.

That asymmetry — $+\$1{,}325$ for defending versus $-\$166{,}500$ for failing to — is the entire case for why **availability and the realistic commitment policy, not the dispatch arithmetic, are where the margin and the risk actually live.** It is also the single thing the flat-swap model is structurally incapable of showing you.

## I.13 Module A in one paragraph

Replace $(F - \mathrm{SRMC}) \times$ flat volume with: **merchant energy margin at the generation-weighted capture price, plus cap-premium carry, minus expected cap payouts, minus commitment drag** — all computed on 5-minute joint power–gas paths whose level is calibrated to the swap forward and whose tail is calibrated to the \$300 cap forward, dispatched under a forecast-driven (non-anticipative) commitment policy that respects start cost, min up/down and ramp so that optionality is not overstated. The swap frame mis-specifies both the *shape* of the hedge and the *reference price* of the margin; the cap frame fixes both and surfaces the uncovered-tail risk that is the dominant economic fact about a NEM peaker.

