# PART IV — MODULE D: Position inference & calibration

*Module C says what generators **should** do. This part recovers what they **did**, from public data, so the supply curve can be **calibrated** rather than asserted. Three estimators of increasing rigour, then reconciliation, then the loop that closes.*

## IV.11 Step 11 — Estimator 1: financial-disclosure regression (company-level net hedge ratio)

Applicable to listed gentailers (AGL, Origin) and government generators (Snowy, Stanwell, CS Energy) that publish AASB 9/7 derivative notes.

### Realised-price regression

Across several reporting halves:

$$\frac{R_t}{Q_t} = a + b\,\bar S_t + \varepsilon_t$$

where $R_t$ = disclosed wholesale revenue, $Q_t$ = generation (from accounts and/or AEMO), $\bar S_t$ = period pool average.

- $b \to 1$: revenue tracks pool → **unhedged**.
- $b \to 0$: revenue flat regardless of pool → **heavily hedged**.
- **Hedge cover $\approx 1 - b$.**

Use HAC / Newey–West standard errors — the residuals are serially correlated across reporting periods and the sample is short, so naive OLS standard errors will overstate precision.

### Cash-flow hedge reserve movement

A large negative movement in the hedge reserve when forwards rose implies a net short forward position:

$$\Delta\text{reserve} \approx (\text{net sold notional}) \times (\text{forward move})$$

summed over the curve, gives a rough net notional.

### Disclosed notional by maturity band

Read directly where available.

### Output and limitations

**Output:** company-level *net* hedge ratio and its trend.

**Limitations — why this is the weakest leg for caps specifically:**

- It is the **net** of generation-sold minus retail-bought.
- It **aggregates** swaps, caps, options, gas and FX into one number.
- It is **half-yearly** — period averaging washes out the 5-minute spikes that caps live in.

Good for baseload/swap coverage and the company trend; poor for cap cover specifically. **Use it as the structural prior, not the answer.**

## IV.12 Step 12 — Estimator 2: revealed defence from dispatch and bids (per-unit, lower bound)

Cap hedging reveals itself in *behaviour*, and behaviour is public at unit level.

**The logic.** A generator short caps must defend them, so it offers capacity at low prices in tight conditions to guarantee dispatch when price runs above \$300 — "reliability-seeking" bidding. An unhedged merchant peaker withholds or offers high to capture the spike.

**From AEMO NEMWEB** — per-DUID dispatch every 5 minutes; price-quantity offer bands, with a publication delay — compute for each unit:

- **Defended volume:** in scarcity / high-RRP intervals, the capacity offered *below* \$300. A revealed **lower bound** on cap cover.
- **Spike capture rate:** the fraction of $S > 300$ intervals where the unit ran near full.

**Output:** per-unit revealed minimum cap cover — the historical, real-data analogue of the spike-capture panel in Module B (Tier 3.2). Units that consistently defend are your cap sellers; the defended MW is the unit estimate.

## IV.13 Step 13 — Estimator 3: bid-markup inversion (the rigorous link from data to a quantity)

The cleanest theoretical route from public data to a hedge quantity, and the most assumption-hungry. **Treat its output as an estimate with a confidence band, not a number.**

### The theory (Allaz–Vila / Green / Wolak)

A firm with fixed-price contract cover $\bar q$ bids to maximise

$$\Pi = P\,q - C(q) + (P_c - P)\,\bar q$$

Offering more depresses price along its residual demand $P(q)$. The first-order condition is the contracts-adjusted Lerner relation:

$$P - \mathrm{SRMC}(q) = -(q - \bar q)\,P'(q), \qquad P'(q) = \frac{1}{DR'(P)} < 0$$

Inverting for contract cover:

$$\boxed{\;\bar q = q - \big(P - \mathrm{SRMC}\big)\,\big|DR'(P)\big|\;}$$

**Read-off.** A **fully contracted** firm ($\bar q = q$ at the margin) bids at marginal cost — zero markup, because it has already locked in revenue and just wants to be dispatched. An **under-contracted** firm with market power bids *above* SRMC ($\bar q < q$), and the size of that markup, scaled by the residual-demand slope, reveals *how* under-contracted it is.

### The inputs

| Input | Source |
|---|---|
| $q$ — dispatch | AEMO NEMWEB, per DUID, 5-minute |
| $P$ — clearing price | AEMO NEMWEB RRP |
| $\mathrm{SRMC}$ | your unit cost model (§I.8) |
| $\vert DR'(P)\vert$ — residual-demand slope magnitude at the clearing point | reconstruct the bid stack: total demand minus *all other participants'* offers; read the change in net residual quantity per \$ around the clearing price |

### The cap-isolation refinement — the important part

A cap is not a simple fixed $\bar q$. It adds to the effective contract position **only when it binds**. So run the inversion in two price regimes:

- **Below \$300:** the cap is dormant. The recovered $\bar q$ = **swap / baseload** contract cover.
- **Above \$300** (spike intervals): the cap binds. The recovered $\bar q_{\text{eff}}$ = swap **+ cap** cover.

$$\boxed{\;\widehat{q}_{cap} = \bar q_{\text{eff}}\big|_{P \ge 300} - \bar q\big|_{P < 300}\;}$$

**The difference isolates the cap volume** — exactly what financial disclosures cannot give you, because they net everything. This below/above split is what turns a generic "contract cover" estimator into a *cap-cover* estimator, and it is the single most valuable trick in the inference layer.

### Confidence band

Bootstrap the two assumption-hungry inputs — the residual-demand slope and SRMC — and report the interval:

- perturb $\vert DR'(P)\vert$ multiplicatively (e.g. 20% relative SD),
- perturb SRMC additively (e.g. \$8/MWh SD, reflecting gas and heat-rate uncertainty),
- re-run the inversion; report mean and 5th/95th percentiles of $\widehat{q}_{cap}$.

### Caveats to state honestly

The slope is noisy; the FOC assumes profit-maximising best-response behaviour that not every participant follows; and in intervals where a unit is constrained-on or constrained-off by a network constraint, the residual-demand construction is invalid and those intervals should be dropped. In practice, filter to intervals where the unit is unconstrained and marginal.

## IV.14 Step 14 — Reconciliation (the cross-checks that bound the estimates)

Triangulate the three estimators against each other and against hard aggregate ceilings.

**1. Company netting check.** Per-unit cap + swap cover (Steps 12–13) summed across a company's fleet should reconcile, roughly, with that company's disclosed **net** notional (Step 11) *once retail is netted off*. Material divergence flags an error in one leg.

**2. Open-interest ceiling.** Fleet-total inferred cap cover **cannot exceed ASX Energy cap open interest** by quarter and region, net of retailer longs and speculative positions:

$$\sum_g \widehat{q}_{cap,g} \;\le\; \mathrm{OI}_{cap} - (\text{retailer long}) - (\text{spec long})$$

**OI is the hard aggregate ceiling and the primary sanity bound.** It is also the cheapest high-value check in the entire build — a breach is dispositive, requires no further analysis, and will catch errors in the inference layer faster than anything else. Build it early.

**3. Regulator context.** AER quarterly wholesale-market reports and occasional ACCC inquiry data give fleet-level hedging context to bound the estimates further.

These three are **the credibility gate**. An inferred position set that breaches the OI ceiling or fails the company-netting reconciliation is wrong, full stop.

## IV.15 Step 15 — The calibration loop (close it)

This is what ties Module C to Module D and separates the framework from an academic exercise.

> Tune the per-owner risk parameters ($\lambda_o$, $\gamma_o$) and the binding constraints (collateral budget, mandate bands) in the Module C reservation/supply model so that the hedging it **predicts** at observed market premiums, $q_{cap,o}^*(p_{\text{screen}})$, matches the hedging you **inferred** actually happened in Steps 12–14.

Concretely, solve the inverse problem

$$\min_{\{\lambda_o,\ \gamma_o,\ \text{constraints}\}} \ \sum_o \Big( q_{cap,o}^*(p_{\text{screen}}) - \widehat{q}_{cap,o} \Big)^2$$

subject to the OI and reconciliation bounds of Step 14, where $\widehat q$ are the inferred actuals.

### Where the loop is well-posed, and where it is not

A practical subtlety the reference implementation surfaces. At a premium *above* actuarial fair value, a peaker sells to its deliverable cap almost regardless of risk aversion — cap-selling is unambiguously risk-reducing *and* profitable, so $\gamma$ saturates and calibration cannot pull the predicted position down. **The risk parameter is only well-identified in the sub-fair-value regime**, where selling is purely risk-reducing and the quantity genuinely trades off against tail exposure. Calibrate there.

### The residual is the diagnostic — do not paper over it

If, after tuning, the residuals stay **large and biased**, that is not a failure of fitting; it is information. It means the reservation-price framework is missing something structural. The usual suspects:

- **Mechanical / policy hedging** that doesn't optimise against price at all: mandated hedge ratios ("be 70% hedged two years out"), refinancing-driven hedging, treasury programmes on a schedule.
- **Binding non-price constraints**: collateral capacity, liquidity in that quarter/region, board concentration limits.
- **Deliberate merchant views** — a participant holding open capacity because it *wants* the spike exposure.

When an inferred position sits below what risk preferences alone predict, the gap is a constraint or a view, not a $\lambda$ to be tuned. Step 15 should be built to *expose* that residual, and to report which of the three explanations the constraint diagnostics point to.

This makes the supply curve **empirically disciplined** rather than a theoretical assertion — the same role that the cap-reprice check (§I.11) plays for the margin model. **A supply curve that cannot reproduce observed hedging behaviour has no standing to predict future behaviour.**

## IV.16 On inferring hedge positions from earnings reports specifically

A recurring temptation is to read hedge volumes straight off financial statements. The honest summary of what that gives you:

- Financials give the **net company hedge ratio** and its trend. That is genuinely useful as a prior and as a reconciliation target.
- They are **poor for caps specifically**, because the disclosure is netted (generation short minus retail long), aggregated across instrument types, and reported half-yearly — and cap value lives in 5-minute spikes that a half-year average annihilates.
- The better routes for caps are **physical**: AEMO dispatch and bidding behaviour (Step 12, revealed defence) and the markup inversion (Step 13), because both operate at the interval resolution where caps actually bind.
- **Open interest** is not a position estimate for any individual participant; it is an *aggregate ceiling*. Use it as a bound, never as an allocation.

The right architecture is therefore: financials as the structural prior, physical/bidding evidence as the per-unit estimator, OI as the ceiling, and the calibration loop to reconcile all three against what the supply-curve model predicts.

