Peakers reservation-sell *below* $\mathbb{E}[\bar C] = \$10.20$; the gentailer's buy reservation sits *above* it — it pays up for insurance. High $\rho^2_{cap}$ with low $\rho^2_{swap}$ is the quantitative "the cap matches the asset" result.

**Steps 8–10 — supply curves and clearing.** QP supply curves rise smoothly from zero and saturate at deliverable capacity, agreeing in shape with the closed-form mean-variance overlay (the QP pulls supply in slightly earlier, as expected from the tail term). At a \$16 screen: fleet supply 497 MW against demand 774 MW; predicted clearing \$17.8/MWh at 500 MW. Cap risk premium $p_{\text{screen}} - \mathbb{E}[\bar C] = +\$5.80$/MWh; implied $\lambda = -1.14\times10^{-7}$, the negative sign confirming the screen sits above actuarial fair value.

