# PART VI — Cross-cutting: pitfalls, gates, data and build order

## VI.1 The pitfalls that actually change answers

| # | Pitfall | Consequence | Fix |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **Variance on the cap leg** | over-predicts hedging supply; understates tail risk | CVaR (§III.3). Single most consequential modelling choice in Module C |
| 2 | **Fleet-wide joint MVO** instead of summed per-owner solves | nets exposures across agents who cannot hedge each other | horizontal aggregation (§III.9) |
| 3 | **Per-asset $M$** instead of netted portfolio | misses the retail/renewable offset that defines gentailer cap behaviour; predicts the biggest players on the wrong side | netted $M_o$ (§III.1) |
| 4 | **Perfect-foresight dispatch** | inflates value by the amount of optionality you don't have | non-anticipative policy (§I.7) |
| 5 | **Netting lumpy costs ex post** while dispatching on SRMC | wrong run *pattern*, not just wrong total | DP/UC with commitment state (§I.7.3) |
| 6 | **Independent gas and power simulation** | misses the co-spike that compresses defence exactly when caps pay | joint simulation (§I.8, §0.3) |
| 7 | **Single unconditional covariance matrix** | "error-maximised" weights; the correlations that matter blow out in stress | condition $\Sigma$ on regime (§III.11) |
| 8 | **Selling caps at nameplate** | manufactures the uncovered tail | $\bar q_{cap}$ = capture-rate haircut (§I.3, §III.5) |
| 9 | **Conflating \$300 strike with the \$600 APC** | mis-specified payoff and mis-specified rails | §I.6 |
| 10 | **Assuming $\lambda$** rather than backing it out | an asserted curve with no empirical standing | calibrate to inferred actuals (§IV.15) |
| 11 | **Treating inferred positions as fact** | false precision on the most assumption-hungry estimator | report bands; lean on physical routes for caps (§IV.13) |
| 12 | **Ignoring the demand side** | you have only the supply blade; can't predict the transacted level | retailer demand curve (§III.9) |
| 13 | **Ignoring MLF on the cap leg** | "covered" positions look like a perfect \$300 lock when they leak $(1-L)S$ | §I.4 |
| 14 | **Staleness corrections on marked back contracts** | can manufacture the comovement you were removing | §I.9 caveat |

## VI.2 Consolidated validation gates

Nothing downstream is trustworthy until these pass.

**Module A (valuation):**
- Cap reprice: simulated cap distribution reproduces traded $p_Q$ under the pricing measure.
- Swap reprice: time-weighted simulated mean reproduces $F_Q$.
- Exceedance curve: simulated $P(S > x)$ matches historical above \$300, truncated at MPC.
- Bound check: realistic-policy value $\le$ constrained-SDP $\le$ unconstrained strip.
- Defence sanity: modelled spike behaviour is physically achievable for the unit.

**Module C (supply curve):**
- Supply curve monotone increasing in price and bounded by deliverable capacity.
- QP solution agrees in shape with the closed-form mean-variance overlay.
- Retailer buy-reservations sit *above* $\mathbb{E}[\bar C]$; generator sell-reservations *below* it.

**Module D (inference):**
- **OI ceiling never breached** by summed inferred or predicted cap supply.
- Company-netting reconciliation within tolerance.
- Markup inversion recovers a planted position on synthetic data before being trusted on real data.
- Calibration residual small and unbiased — or, if not, attributed to a named constraint rather than absorbed into $\lambda$.

