# APPENDIX C — The whole thing in one paragraph

Value each NEM gas generator as **merchant energy margin at its generation-weighted capture price, plus cap-premium carry, minus expected cap payouts, minus commitment drag** — computed on 5-minute joint power–gas paths whose level is calibrated to the ASX swap and whose tail is calibrated to the \$300 cap, dispatched under a forecast-driven non-anticipative commitment policy so optionality is not overstated, and reported as a *distribution* whose left tail is the uncovered-spike risk that dominates the economics. Present it to the desk decision-first across seven tiers, keeping risk-neutral fair value and physical expected margin explicitly separate, because their gap *is* the risk premium being earned. Then, to predict where hedges will actually trade, take each **owner's netted** portfolio margin — thermal plus renewables minus retail, which is what flips integrated gentailers from cap sellers to cap buyers — and solve a **per-owner mean-variance-CVaR program** under deliverable-capacity, collateral, mandate and liquidity constraints; sweep the offered price to trace that owner's reservation-price-as-a-function-of-volume, sum **horizontally** across owners rather than solving the fleet jointly, add the symmetric retailer demand side, and read the clearing premium against the ASX screen via the implied-$\lambda$ back-out. Finally, **calibrate the whole apparatus** by inferring what generators actually hedged — company net ratios from financial disclosures as the structural prior, per-unit cap defence from AEMO dispatch and bids, and per-unit cap cover from a **markup inversion run separately below and above \$300** to isolate caps from swaps — reconciled against open interest as the hard aggregate ceiling, tuning each owner's risk aversion in the sub-fair-value regime where it is identified, and treating any large biased residual not as fitting error but as evidence of mechanical hedging or a binding non-price constraint. The result is a margin model that surfaces the risk the flat-swap frame structurally cannot show, and a hedging supply curve that is theoretically grounded *and* empirically disciplined — turning the ASX screen into a read on whether caps are cheap or rich relative to where the fleet will actually supply them.
